        "id": "ch15_6-3",
        "text": "PowerShell 7 and the Raspberry Pi\n382\nIn this loop, we\u2019re going to need to turn the LED on and off using Set-GpioPin . If you followed\nthe preceding diagram, you should be using pin 17.\nWe\u2019ll also need to use Start-Sleep to wait between each command; otherwise, we\u2019ll blow the LED up:\nImport-Module Microsoft.PowerShell.IoT\nwhile ($true) {\nSet-GpioPin -Id 17 -Value \"High\"\nWrite-Host \"LED on\"\nStart-Sleep 1\nSet-GpioPin -Id 17 -Value \"Low\"\nWrite-Host \"LED off\"\nStart-Sleep 1\n}\nI\u2019ve added a couple of output lines here so that I can reasonably demonstrate this without using a\nYouTube link. Running it looks like this in VS Code:\nFigure 15.18 \u2013 Blinkin\u2019 LEDs\nSummary\n383\nAs we can see, the LED\u2019s state changes once a second; this will continue until the script is stopped manually.\nWe can see how it compares to the programs we saw for the Arduino, in C, and the Python program.\nThere\u2019s also an example LED module in the Examples folder \u2013 it\u2019s well worth taking a look to see\nhow they\u2019ve done it in there.\nThat about wraps it up for this chapter. Let\u2019s summarize what we\u2019ve learned.",
